
A context read written inside a slot population belongs to the caller, so the
context lowering has to see it there -- the reachability scan, the call-edge
scan and the provider threading each walked a NodeInst's children and skipped
its Slots. Reading the default value is slot_context_read_read.txtar; here a
provider wraps the call site.

The read is lexically the caller's, so it resolves against the provider the
caller sits under and not the context's default. Threading that walked
Children alone would put "light" here, which is a wrong answer rather than a
crash -- so this is the archive where the absence is the assertion, and html
alone is enough to show it.

deny out/none/html/index.html `light` -- the provider's "dark" is what reaches the slot; the context's default never appears
-- provided.sngl --
import . "sngl:ui"

output {
    none {
        html
    }
}

context #theme("light")

component framed(caption component) {
    caption {
        text(value="(none)")
    }
}

component main {
    theme("dark") {
        framed() {
            component caption {
                text(value=theme)
            }
        }
    }
}
-- out/none/html/index.html --
<!-- Code generated by sngl (html) from provided.sngl; DO NOT EDIT. -->

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html><head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <style>
* { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
body { font-family: system-ui, sans-serif; }
  </style>
</head><body>

<span>dark</span>

<script>
(() => {
  // sngl-entry.js
  var state = {};
})();
</script>
</body></html>
